      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-09-10T17:35:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It depends. When unmapping pages, TLB flush is triggered only by pKVM. KVM-high just issues the unmap hypercall and relies on pKVM to flush the TLB, as a part of pkvm_pgtable_unmap(). Which is why, for instance, in pkvm_unmap_gfn_range() in aosp/3684270 we always return false, which indicates to the caller that it doesn\u0027t need to flush TLB, since it has been flushed by pKVM.\n\nSimilarly when changing permissions of mapped pages (although in this initial pvMMU implementation we don\u0027t really allow changing permissions, for simplicity, since we don\u0027t seem to have use cases for that; but when we support it, I think we will rely on pKVM to do the flush, like in the unmap case).\n\nIn contrast, when clearing the access bit in the PTE (in the age hypercall implemented in aosp/3700490), pKVM does not flush the TLB. KVM-high triggers flushing the TLB after that, if needed, depending on which MMU notifier triggered it: .clear_young() or .clear_flush_young().\n\nIn such case, when KVM-high triggers TLB flush, it ends up using a hypercall \\_\\_pkvm\\_\\_flush_tlb_all or \\_\\_pkvm\\_\\_flush_tlb_current etc, which you kindly implemented in pvVMCS.\n\nPossibly there are also some other code paths in KVM-high that still trigger TLB flush via those hypercalls, maybe redundantly. TBH, I haven\u0027t analyzed all those code paths very carefully.",
      "parentUuid": "34ebdad6_6926be93",
      "revId": "ee507d4ad2d412ccff9345832ee0b73340962d95",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
